<Base title="Story & Narrative"><section class="interior"><div class="eyebrow">WORK / NARRATIVE BUILDING</div><h1>Telling Your Story</h1><p class="lede">Narrative and positioning for people and organizations in transition.</p><div class="callout"><h2>Finding the thread that connects it all.</h2><p>A career spanning 10, 15, or 20 years can be surprisingly difficult to explain in a few sentences. The same is true of a business that has evolved beyond its original purpose. Often, the challenge isn’t figuring out what you’ve done. It’s finding the thread that connects it all.</p><p>Through conversation, reflection, and a few well-placed questions, we uncover the story that’s already there and find language that feels both authentic and useful.</p><p>When a leader takes on a new role, a business evolves beyond its original offering, or an organization changes direction, the old way of describing the work often stops fitting.</p><p>I use interviews, stories, and pointed questions to uncover what is actually changing, what is distinctive, and what the audience needs to understand.</p></div><div class="callout"><h2>Questions worth asking</h2><p>What do people come to you for that they struggle to name? Which story do you keep telling because it explains your work better than a title? What has changed that your current language hasn't caught up with? Who needs to understand the next chapter, and what do they need to believe?</p></div><div class="callout"><h2>What takes shape</h2><p>A clearer central narrative, language that sounds natural when spoken, and a set of themes that can carry across conversations, presentations, introductions, and websites. The output depends on the moment and the audience, not a standard personal-brand formula.</p></div><p><a href="/work/">← Back to Work</a></p></section></Base>
